From: UgoHTTP <noreply@ugohttp.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] socketpair: disable Nagle on the TCP wakeup pair

Copyright (C) Daniel Stenberg, <daniel@haxx.se>, et al.
SPDX-License-Identifier: curl

Targets curl 8.23.0 (lib/socketpair.c).

Defect: without eventfd, pipe and socketpair, curl_multi_wakeup() writes
one byte to a TCP connection over loopback (wakeup_inet). Nagle holds a
write back while an earlier one is unacknowledged, and the receiving
stack acknowledges late (delayed ACK), so a wakeup that follows another
one reaches curl_multi_poll() about 100 ms later. Repro on Nintendo
Switch: abort a transfer that stalls after its headers; the abort is
delivered about 95 ms later instead of within a millisecond.

Fix: set TCP_NODELAY on socks[1], the end Curl_wakeup_signal() writes to,
once the pair is verified.

Caught by: no test in this repository: Linux, Android and Apple wake with
eventfd or a pipe, and Windows loopback acknowledges at once. Seen on
Nintendo Switch, where an abort took 95 ms and takes about 1 ms patched.

Drop: when curl sets TCP_NODELAY on its wakeup pair itself.
---
--- a/lib/socketpair.c
+++ b/lib/socketpair.c
@@ -148,6 +148,9 @@
 #ifdef HAVE_ARPA_INET_H
 #include <arpa/inet.h>
 #endif
+#ifdef HAVE_NETINET_TCP_H
+#include <netinet/tcp.h>
+#endif
 
 #ifndef INADDR_LOOPBACK
 #define INADDR_LOOPBACK 0x7f000001
@@ -267,6 +270,15 @@
     } while(1);
   }
 
+#ifdef TCP_NODELAY
+  {
+    /* Every wakeup is a one-byte write: with Nagle, a second one waits for
+       the peer's delayed ACK of the first. */
+    int nodelay = 1;
+    (void)setsockopt(socks[1], IPPROTO_TCP, TCP_NODELAY, (void *)&nodelay,
+                     (curl_socklen_t)sizeof(nodelay));
+  }
+#endif
   if(nonblocking)
     if(curlx_nonblock(socks[0], TRUE) < 0 ||
        curlx_nonblock(socks[1], TRUE) < 0)
